1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5
Be6 9. Qe2 Be7 10. c4 {"a move earlier than usual"} bxc4 11. Ba4 ({Here,
instead of playing the simple} 11. Bxc4 {, which was the original idea of the
line, White went astray with the swashbuckling 11 Ba4? If 11 Bxc4} Qc8 ({
if, as Penrose suggested} 11... dxc4 12. Qxe4 Qd5 13. Qf4 {, with an open
position where White clearly stands better. I leave further lines to our
opening analysts. (n.b. analysis engines think Black stands better in this
line - JS)}) 12. Rd1 {, with a position very similar to the game Keres-Euwe in
the World Championship.}) 11... Bd7 12. e6 fxe6 13. Bxc6 Bxc6 14. Ne5 Qd6 15.
Qh5+ g6 16. Nxg6 Nf6 17. Qh3 Rg8 18. Bf4 Qc5 19. Nxe7 Qxe7 20. Bxc7 Rg6 {
Black has now the better game and won after a long ending. The value of the
innovation, however, is not settled by this game as White seems to stand well
after the correct 11 Bxc4.} 21. Bg3 Bd7 22. Qh4 Ne4 23. Qh5 Qg5 24. Qxg5 Rxg5
25. Nc3 Nxg3 26. hxg3 Rb8 27. Rab1 Ke7 28. Rfe1 Kf6 29. f3 Rxg3 30. Kf2 Rbg8
31. Rg1 R3g7 32. b3 Rc8 33. Rgc1 cxb3 34. Rxb3 Rc6 35. Rb7 Be8 36. Rb8 Rgc7 37.
Rxe8 Rxc3 38. Rf8+ Kg7 39. Rxc3 Rxc3 40. Re8 Rc2+ 41. Kg3 Kf6 42. Rh8 Rxa2 43.
Rxh7 a5 44. f4 a4 45. Ra7 a3 46. Ra8 Ra1 47. Kg4 a2 48. Ra6 Ke7 49. Kg5 d4 50.
Ra7+ Kd6 51. Ra3 Kc5 52. Kf6 Kb4 53. Ra8 d3 54. Rb8+ Kc3 55. Rc8+ Kd4 56. Ra8
d2 57. Rd8+ Ke3 0-1

